Since the number of positive divisors of a positive integer $m$ does not exceed $2\sqrt{m}$, and $a_1, \dots, a_k$ are pairwise distinct positive divisors of $[a_1, \dots, a_k]$, it follows that $[a_1, \dots, a_k] \ge k^2/4$. Consequently,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{1}{[a_1, \dots, a_k]} &= \frac{1}{a_1} + \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{1}{[a_1, \dots, a_k]} 
\le 1 + \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{4}{k^2} 
< 1 + 4 \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{1}{k^2 - \frac{1}{4}} \\
&= 1 + 4 \cdot 2 \left( \frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{2n+1} \right) < \frac{11}{3} < 4.
\end{aligned}
$$

